      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(infra): bound response body read in non-streaming fallback paths",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`readResponseBodySnippet` in `src/infra/http-error-body.ts` has a non-streaming fallback path that calls `response.text()` without any size limit, potentially buffering an entire provider error response into memory before the `maxBytes` check runs. Similarly, `readResponsePrefix` in `src/infra/http-body.ts` has a no-reader fallback that calls `response.arrayBuffer()` without bounds.\n\nThis can exhaust Gateway memory when a provider returns an oversized error body.\n\nFixes #101269\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n1. **Export `readResponsePrefix`** and reuse it in `readResponseBodySnippet` instead of the unbounded `response.text()` path\n2. **Add Content-Length guard to `readResponsePrefix`** — when no ReadableStream reader is available, check `Content-Length` header before calling `arrayBuffer()` to avoid materializing oversized bodies\n3. **Add null body early return** — when `body` is null, return immediately without calling `arrayBuffer()`\n4. **Add tests** for Content-Length gating and null body handling\n\n## User Impact\n\nGateway error-path memory usage is now bounded at `maxBytes` regardless of whether the response body is a ReadableStream or a plain object. No API or behavior change for callers.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 24/24 unit tests pass (11 http-error-body + 13 http-body)\n- Format and lint pass (`oxfmt`, `oxlint`)\n- Real behavior proof passes on CI\n- Based on PR #101270 by @Pandah97, with added lint fixes (`Number.parseInt`/`Number.isNaN`)\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
